cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Placebo-Controlled Estimate of Comprehension Failure in Reported LLM Defection
message: >-
  If you use this software or the released databases, please cite them using
  the metadata in this file.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Uday
    family-names: Gattu
    email: udaygattu007@gmail.com
repository-code: 'https://github.com/UdayGattu/comprehension-defection'
url: 'https://github.com/UdayGattu/comprehension-defection'
abstract: >-
  Six experiments testing whether defection by large language models in the
  Iterated Prisoner's Dilemma is caused by failure to track the game state.
  A token- and density-matched placebo arm separates the effect of the state
  block's presence from the effect of its content, and comprehension is measured
  directly by probing the model for each state field. Across three open-weight
  instruct models (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct,
  Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3), two readouts (constrained next-token and
  chain-of-thought), three label framings and up to six arms, the state is
  available, readable at a comprehension probe rate of 1.000 in the treatment
  arm, and preferentially read over the raw history - and the pre-registered
  opponent-conditional prediction is rejected in every valid group. Field-level
  falsification isolates the opponent's last move, not the cumulative score, as
  the operative field. The release includes the pre-registration, every
  experiment's SQLite database, and the analysis scripts that regenerate every
  quoted interval.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - iterated prisoner's dilemma
  - game theory
  - state tracking
  - comprehension
  - placebo control
  - pre-registration
  - reproducibility
  - Llama
  - Qwen
  - Mistral
